For a two-body decay at rest with a massless neutrino:

\[
K_\mu = \frac{(M-m)^2}{2M}
\]

\[
K_\nu = \frac{M^2-m^2}{2M}
\]

Given:

\[
M = 139.6\ \text{MeV}, \quad m = 105.7\ \text{MeV}
\]

Muon kinetic energy:

\[
K_\mu = \frac{(139.6-105.7)^2}{2(139.6)}
= \frac{(33.9)^2}{279.2}
\approx 4.12\ \text{MeV}
\]

Neutrino kinetic energy:

\[
K_\nu = \frac{139.6^2 - 105.7^2}{2(139.6)}
\approx 29.8\ \text{MeV}
\]

So the kinetic energies are approximately \(4.12\ \text{MeV}\) and \(29.8\ \text{MeV}\).

Answer: B
